WEBVTT

00:00.477 --> 00:02.215
- [Instructor] Olga was asked to find

00:02.215 --> 00:05.263
where f of x is equal to x
minus two to the fourth power

00:05.263 --> 00:06.667
has inflection points.

00:06.667 --> 00:08.379
This is her solution.

00:08.379 --> 00:10.134
So we look at her solution
and then they ask us,

00:10.134 --> 00:12.170
is Olga's work correct?

00:12.170 --> 00:14.292
If not, what's her mistake?

00:14.292 --> 00:17.428
So pause this video and see
if you can figure this out.

00:17.428 --> 00:19.219
Alright, let's just follow her work.

00:19.219 --> 00:21.664
So here, she's trying to
take the first derivative.

00:21.664 --> 00:23.093
So you would apply the chain rule.

00:23.093 --> 00:26.230
It would be four times x
minus two to the third power

00:26.230 --> 00:29.165
times the derivative of x
minus two which is just one.

00:29.165 --> 00:30.998
So this checks out.

00:30.998 --> 00:32.265
Then you take the derivative of this.

00:32.265 --> 00:34.340
It'd be three times four which would be 12

00:34.340 --> 00:36.496
times x minus two to the second power

00:36.496 --> 00:38.694
times the derivative of x
minus two which is just one

00:38.694 --> 00:40.360
which is exactly what she has here,

00:40.360 --> 00:42.848
12 times x minus two to the second power.

00:42.848 --> 00:46.151
That checks out so step
one's looking good for Olga.

00:46.151 --> 00:49.539
Step two, the solution
of the second derivative

00:49.539 --> 00:51.946
equaling zero is x equals two.

00:51.946 --> 00:53.046
That looks right.

00:53.046 --> 00:56.835
The second derivative is 12
times x minus two squared

00:56.835 --> 00:58.992
and we wanna make that equal to zero.

00:58.992 --> 01:02.623
This is only going to be
true when x is equal to two.

01:02.623 --> 01:05.155
So step two is looking good.

01:05.155 --> 01:09.171
So step three, Olga says
f has an inflection point

01:09.171 --> 01:10.743
at x equals two.

01:10.743 --> 01:13.343
So she's basing this just on the fact

01:13.343 --> 01:16.364
that the second derivative is
zero when x is equal to two.

01:16.364 --> 01:18.205
She's basing this just on the fact

01:18.205 --> 01:21.513
that f prime prime of
two is equal to zero.

01:21.513 --> 01:23.391
Now, I have a problem with this

01:23.391 --> 01:25.593
because the fact that
your second derivative

01:25.593 --> 01:26.983
is zero at x equals two,

01:26.983 --> 01:29.594
that makes two a nice
candidate to check out

01:29.594 --> 01:31.224
but you can't immediately say

01:31.224 --> 01:33.591
that we have an inflection point there.

01:33.591 --> 01:35.657
Remember, an inflection
point is where we go

01:35.657 --> 01:38.194
from being concave upwards
to concave downwards

01:38.194 --> 01:40.475
or concave downwards to concave upwards

01:40.475 --> 01:42.634
and speaking in the language
of the second derivative,

01:42.634 --> 01:45.689
it means that the second
derivative changes signs

01:45.689 --> 01:50.336
as we go from below x equals
two to above x equals two

01:50.336 --> 01:51.560
but we have to test that

01:51.560 --> 01:54.123
because it's not
necessarily always the case.

01:54.123 --> 01:56.159
So let's actually test it.

01:56.159 --> 01:59.056
Let's think about some intervals.

01:59.056 --> 01:59.889
Intervals.

02:00.888 --> 02:02.519
So let's think about the interval

02:02.519 --> 02:05.130
when we go from negative infinity to two

02:05.130 --> 02:06.069
and let's think about the interval

02:06.069 --> 02:08.636
where we go from two to positive infinity.

02:08.636 --> 02:13.163
If you want, you could
have some test values.

02:13.163 --> 02:16.137
You could think about the sign,

02:16.137 --> 02:18.554
sign of our second derivative

02:20.063 --> 02:23.976
and then based on that, you
could think about concavity,

02:23.976 --> 02:25.226
concavity of f.

02:28.093 --> 02:29.966
So let's think about what's happening.

02:29.966 --> 02:31.312
So you could take a test value.

02:31.312 --> 02:33.474
Let's say one is in this interval

02:33.474 --> 02:35.309
and let's say three is in this interval

02:35.309 --> 02:39.637
and you could say one minus
two squared is going to be,

02:39.637 --> 02:41.819
let's see, that's negative
one squared which is one

02:41.819 --> 02:44.585
and then you're just going to,
this is just going to be 12

02:44.585 --> 02:48.090
so this is going to be positive
and if you tried three,

02:48.090 --> 02:50.781
three minus two squared is one times 12.

02:50.781 --> 02:52.534
Well, that's also going to be positive

02:52.534 --> 02:55.591
and so you're going to be concave upwards.

02:55.591 --> 02:57.177
At least in these test values,

02:57.177 --> 03:00.002
it looks like on either side of two

03:00.002 --> 03:01.291
that the sign of the second derivative

03:01.291 --> 03:03.163
is positive on either side
of the two and you might say,

03:03.163 --> 03:05.892
well, maybe I just need
to find closer values

03:05.892 --> 03:08.094
but if you inspect the
second derivative here,

03:08.094 --> 03:10.335
you can see that this is
never going to be negative.

03:10.335 --> 03:14.028
In fact, for any value
other than x equals two

03:14.028 --> 03:15.930
and this value right
over here since we're,

03:15.930 --> 03:18.868
even if x minus two is
negative, you're squaring it

03:18.868 --> 03:20.660
which will make this entire thing positive

03:20.660 --> 03:22.899
and then multiplying it
times a positive value.

03:22.899 --> 03:26.409
So for any value other than x equals two,

03:26.409 --> 03:28.860
the sign of our second
derivative is positive

03:28.860 --> 03:32.319
which means that we're
going to be concave upwards

03:32.319 --> 03:34.558
and so we actually don't
have an inflection point

03:34.558 --> 03:37.207
at x equals two because
we are not switching signs

03:37.207 --> 03:41.119
as we go from values
less than x equals two

03:41.119 --> 03:43.518
to values greater than x equals two.

03:43.518 --> 03:46.820
Our second derivative
is not switching sides.

03:46.820 --> 03:48.781
So once again, this is incorrect.

03:48.781 --> 03:51.349
We actually don't have an
inflection point at x equals two

03:51.349 --> 03:54.077
because our second derivative
does not switch signs

03:54.077 --> 03:55.831
as we cross x equals two

03:55.831 --> 03:59.331
which means our concavity does not change.
